// ═══ {{ feature }} ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The component's authored tests, sealed behind its Cargo feature. The `#[cfg]` — not the module —
// is what isolates them: an inherent `impl Fixture` contributes its methods to the type GLOBALLY
// (rustc E0592 fires on a duplicate method name even when the two `impl` blocks sit in different
// modules), so a module alone would not stop two components' helpers from colliding. Gating means
// only ONE section's items ever exist in a build, and since each `#[invariant_test]` expands to its
// own `fn main()` + `#[global_allocator]`, exactly one feature is ever enabled. Components can
// therefore define same-named helpers forever without interfering.
//
// The module is the single item the `#[cfg]` hangs on, which is why the section is not gated
// item-by-item: doing that would mean parsing item boundaries out of authored source.
#[cfg(feature = "{{ feature }}")]
mod {{ module }} {
    use super::*;

{{ body }}
}

// The entry point is OURS, generated: `#[invariant_test]` expands `fn main()` as a SIBLING of the
// fn it annotates, so it has to be applied at crate root — the authored fn cannot carry it from
// inside the module. The macro only reads the signature (never the body), so a delegating wrapper
// gets the real harness while the authored code stays sealed above.
#[cfg(feature = "{{ feature }}")]
#[invariant_test]
fn {{ feature }}(fixture: &mut Fixture) {
    {{ module }}::{{ feature }}(fixture)
}
